{
  "id": "komparativ-superlativ",
  "name": "Comparatives and superlatives",
  "level": "A2",
  "status": "geprueft",
  "subtitle": "Use the comparative to compare two things: The bus is cheaper than the taxi. Use the superlative to say which one is number one: The bike is the cheapest. When two things are the same, use as … as.",
  "briefMarks": [
    "cheaper",
    "than",
    "the cheapest",
    "as … as"
  ],
  "source": "EnglishAcademy original English text (grammar-a2 run, 2026-10-29)",
  "concept": {
    "flow": [
      {
        "text": "cheap"
      },
      {
        "text": "cheaper than",
        "variant": "verb"
      },
      {
        "text": "the cheapest",
        "variant": "prep"
      }
    ],
    "exampleHtml": "The bus is <span class=\"vp-verb\">cheaper than</span> the taxi. · The bike is <span class=\"vp-prep\">the cheapest</span>.",
    "qa": [
      {
        "html": "<strong>Short adjectives:</strong> add <em>-er</em> and use <strong>than</strong>. For number one, add <em>-est</em> and use <strong>the</strong>: <em>The new flat is smaller than the old one. It is the smallest flat in the street.</em>",
        "tag": "-er / -est"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>Spelling:</strong> adjective ends in <em>-e</em>: add <em>-r / -st</em> (<em>nice → nicer, the nicest</em>). One vowel + one consonant: double the consonant (<em>big → bigger, the biggest; hot → hotter</em>). Consonant + <em>-y</em>: <em>-y → -ier / -iest</em> (<em>easy → easier, the easiest</em>).",
        "tag": "Spelling"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>Long adjectives</strong> (two or more syllables, for example <em>expensive, interesting, careful</em>): use <strong>more</strong> and <strong>the most</strong>. Do not add <em>-er</em>: <em>This hotel is more expensive than that one. It is the most expensive hotel in town.</em>",
        "tag": "more / most"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>Irregular:</strong> <em>good → better → the best</em>, <em>bad → worse → the worst</em>, <em>far → further → the furthest</em> (<em>farther / the farthest</em> is also correct): <em>My cooking is bad, but his is worse.</em>",
        "tag": "good, bad, far"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>The same:</strong> <em>as</em> + adjective + <em>as</em>: <em>My son is as tall as me.</em> <strong>Not the same:</strong> <em>not as</em> + adjective + <em>as</em>: <em>The bus is not as fast as the train.</em> Or use the comparative: <em>The train is faster than the bus.</em>",
        "tag": "as … as"
      },
      {
        "html": "✗ <em>more cheaper</em> → ✓ <em>cheaper</em> (never <em>more</em> and <em>-er</em> together) · ✗ <em>He is taller then me.</em> → ✓ <em>He is taller than me.</em> · ✗ <em>the most tall</em> → ✓ <em>the tallest</em> · ✗ <em>gooder, the goodest</em> → ✓ <em>better, the best</em>",
        "tag": "Typical mistakes"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "Some two-syllable adjectives can use both forms: <em>cleverer / more clever</em>, <em>quieter / more quiet</em>. Adjectives ending in <em>-y</em> always use <em>-ier</em>: <em>happier, easier</em>. After a superlative, use <em>in</em> with a place or group and <em>of</em> with a number or <em>all</em>: <em>the tallest in the class, the best of all</em>. In everyday speech you can also say <em>as tall as I am</em> or <em>as tall as me</em>.",
